<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yusuf Ali Kenadid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yusuf Ali Kenadid was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> ruler who founded the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobyo">Hobyo</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> coast in the 1870s. A member of the ruling house of the Majeerteen Sultanate, he fought a long war with his kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boqor-osman-mahamuud">Boqor Osman Mahamuud</a> before leaving to carve out a new state further south. In 1888 he accepted an Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over Hobyo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yusuf-ali-kenadid.jpg" alt="Yusuf Ali Kenadid" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luigi Robecchi Bricchetti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Yusuf_Ali_Kenadid_Painting.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yusuf Ali Kenadid was born in 1837 into the Bah Yaaqub branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boqor-osman-mahamuud">Osman Mahamuud</a>, a Majeerteen Darod family. He amassed a fortune of his own and became a skilled military leader. Kenadid was not his surname but a title given to him by his rivals, meaning He who refuses to be haltered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His election as sultan by the Bah Lelkase and Bah Yaqub at Alula began a dynastic conflict within the Majeerteen Sultanate. He failed to seize control of it from his cousin and rival, Boqor Osman Mahamud, and was forced into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. A decade later, in the 1870s, he returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> with a band of Hadhrami led by Husni Al Kathiri, overcame the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habar-gidir">Habar Gidir</a> clans of Mudug and established the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobyo">Hobyo</a> in 1878.</p><p>To protect himself from Zanzibari hostility he signed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, then present in the region only nominally, since British protection from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a> would have limited his autonomy. The treaty recognised him as sovereign along the coast from Ras Awath in the north to Mereeg, though his power was then consolidated on only a short stretch. Italy was to keep out of his administration and gave arms and an annual subsidy in return for limited oversight and economic concessions. The sultanate's expansion inland was his own initiative, and he later persuaded the reluctant Boqor Osman to accept Italian protection as well.</p><p>Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> soured when he refused to let British troops land in his sultanate to pursue the Dervish movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-abdullah-hassan">Mohammed Abdullah Hassan</a>. Seeing him as a threat, the Italians exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and then to Italian Eritrea, together with his son and heir Ali Yusuf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-yusuf-kenadid">Ali Yusuf Kenadid</a> succeeded him as sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobyo">Hobyo</a>. Another son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-yusuf-kenadid">Osman Yusuf Kenadid</a>, created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osmanya">Osmanya script</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> language, and his grandson Yasin Osman Kenadid later helped found the Society for Somali Language and Literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yusuf Ali Kenadid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Boqor Osman Mahamuud fought a long war with his kinsman <b>Yusuf Ali Kenadid</b>, who left to found the Sultanate of Hobyo.</blockquote><a href="/state/majeerteen-sultanate">Majeerteen Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Yusuf Ali Kenadid</b>, a member of the ruling house of the Majeerteen Sultanate, quarrelled with his cousin Boqor Osman Mahamuud and left the north.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-hobyo">Sultanate of Hobyo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Italian Eritrea. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
